 {:db/id "q-335460" :question/id #uuid "d4f31534-294a-5c66-bb7a-bbd9c8ed5e89" :question/math-academy-id "q-335460" :question/problem "Select the correct options in the statement below that describe an **unstable** equilibrium point.{{field-1}} solutions that start\n{{field-2}}\nthe equilibrium\n{{field-3}}\nthe equilibrium." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n$\\text{Some}$ solutions that start $\\text{arbitrarily close to}$ the equilibrium $\\text{eventually move away from}$ the equilibrium." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335460/ma-source-field/44df59e4b9ee60c06dc2e1c10402131b446a935d930b385722eb315bc9eaa22b" "q-335460/ma-source-field/109ace1db91725f42902b45fe82fe5a23e60b9fa2a7bee3adb6df27f7ebae4c1" "q-335460/ma-source-field/ffe1cc674d2a127ef7a878ab5c7500a05020c9c5e01ccb739a50783e576fd8c0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
