 {:db/id "q-337395/ma-source-field/7b533f34df9f2faa96f1cd182165677fbb6813fbd1aa1822acd8518f001e503d" :answer-field/id #uuid "008636f6-a3d9-555d-ad4c-a5efd04935ce" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/choices ["q-337395/ma-source-field/7b533f34df9f2faa96f1cd182165677fbb6813fbd1aa1822acd8518f001e503d/8e732cc329f7a21b783a9b5f2e4ecdafe614533f3fcec24a1a0b1b8b07e7f6de" "q-337395/ma-source-field/7b533f34df9f2faa96f1cd182165677fbb6813fbd1aa1822acd8518f001e503d/c6e6ed44ddcb58c5b75a7f32229d30d20406545ffde31ec1c83097bd5d5f7331" "q-337395/ma-source-field/7b533f34df9f2faa96f1cd182165677fbb6813fbd1aa1822acd8518f001e503d/5dbe2692d5da78cea234d52affbc8317a15891f62d69f5b5d28c5f14b4eb1136" "q-337395/ma-source-field/7b533f34df9f2faa96f1cd182165677fbb6813fbd1aa1822acd8518f001e503d/38e32fe5ded239a97136109e00232b171e3239a6ebe02218452940cf05ee34d9"] :answer-field/correct "q-337395/ma-source-field/7b533f34df9f2faa96f1cd182165677fbb6813fbd1aa1822acd8518f001e503d/38e32fe5ded239a97136109e00232b171e3239a6ebe02218452940cf05ee34d9" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-337395" :question/id #uuid "bc82baf7-6e13-5137-9b62-5ced3f36541b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337395" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y=2x-5.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{x})}^{′}-(u{e}^{x}) & =2x-5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}-u{e}^{x} & =2x-5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{x}+\\cancel{u{e}^{x}}-\\cancel{u{e}^{x}} & =2x-5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{x} & =2x-5 \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-x}(2x-5) \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\int {e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-337395/ma-source-field/d82f787f5e3cc83d62a9c242bc098a262da173391cd35bef0bfbb2facd7f603f" "q-337395/ma-source-field/f350c549b5a5a4fa299e6db09fa37b0e20842cc35986251138f18cabaec62242" "q-337395/ma-source-field/7b533f34df9f2faa96f1cd182165677fbb6813fbd1aa1822acd8518f001e503d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
