 {:db/id "q-337469/ma-source-field/ff9c27228ee2eded91a3fab57e15c90e06d207ea4d6658a527b777f4dfbe2a5b" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d0b526f-0a36-505c-bd42-a28d844cfb3c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/choices ["q-337469/ma-source-field/ff9c27228ee2eded91a3fab57e15c90e06d207ea4d6658a527b777f4dfbe2a5b/0e0362d2084a1dc35fc11954d0a32439da98298eb4fd4b9f0d98c92ed9c2a7ac" "q-337469/ma-source-field/ff9c27228ee2eded91a3fab57e15c90e06d207ea4d6658a527b777f4dfbe2a5b/60172a93ff8563c6527e61cddde086b162debe96b58dc56c2a5f96f012005c19" "q-337469/ma-source-field/ff9c27228ee2eded91a3fab57e15c90e06d207ea4d6658a527b777f4dfbe2a5b/5dbc5a0aedeaaecf9bff216fd115adb3f549c2058284f4910eedf38e95a13c3c" "q-337469/ma-source-field/ff9c27228ee2eded91a3fab57e15c90e06d207ea4d6658a527b777f4dfbe2a5b/38dcd47d19959d51d8fdb19a88fcbf42b9c321773a93a752320fb8e9ac722eeb"] :answer-field/correct "q-337469/ma-source-field/ff9c27228ee2eded91a3fab57e15c90e06d207ea4d6658a527b777f4dfbe2a5b/0e0362d2084a1dc35fc11954d0a32439da98298eb4fd4b9f0d98c92ed9c2a7ac" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-337469" :question/id #uuid "34254624-9c90-5339-836d-8f0fcd78b2d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-337469" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+7y={e}^{-7x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+7{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-7x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-7x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-7x})}^{′}+7(u{e}^{-7x}) & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x}-7u{e}^{-7x}+7u{e}^{-7x} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x}-\\cancel{7u{e}^{-7x}}+\\cancel{7u{e}^{-7x}} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-7x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-7x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{-7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-337469/ma-source-field/4adfba6e2eab8ea14a33b1abd4e02de59b12a7a74e6367e98b30bcd7a4ebb416" "q-337469/ma-source-field/3245619473fccef9b200a8924dd881fc078f265f704023e165682e493019279f" "q-337469/ma-source-field/ff9c27228ee2eded91a3fab57e15c90e06d207ea4d6658a527b777f4dfbe2a5b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
