 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "d7903a61-d4e3-5a7b-991a-79f6efafb8b5"] :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the \n{{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${a}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{odd}$ and the $\\text{cosine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-338682/ma-source-field/74c64641a2eb54858c89f7472cbd906d97a730a2eed168f86c785bc136849c50" "q-338682/ma-source-field/d2ea1b4f0ba852655c4c3ed6ebb9c6d8277c44c0dc8ac3ddd9dcabf3f9970589"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
