 {:db/id "q-338713" :question/id #uuid "9b12a4da-b770-5d48-8104-f0ea5dff8e44" :question/math-academy-id "q-338713" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{′}(x)-2y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi \n$$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(\\pi )=0\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{′}(x)-2y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x≢0,$ the equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(\\pi )=0.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Neumann}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are zero for both, they are $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is inhomogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are homogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-338713/ma-source-field/b416211afb1db3691dc69bd37b794a6a12159a613c3c04f5654bced2573a228d" "q-338713/ma-source-field/35076650840cbb6beacf9f343738a476a3e52b3aadd11f5ca9f98f5a2c7cbdce" "q-338713/ma-source-field/a216a5ca1bb4cd198485d7aef064e21f10e834a6d6f738ebe9755a5d281cc861" "q-338713/ma-source-field/947acceff23d77d1e11fd7a62cf60c6e87947d113418ca9fc39a3f66bd026148"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
