 {:db/id "q-338853/ma-source-field/d737afc49887d8ca51fce1d31ecf2afa4a8d302e354ee0c18592adab10450737" :answer-field/id #uuid "c3f0e357-b57a-5092-8107-004d7ace2a2f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/choices ["q-338853/ma-source-field/d737afc49887d8ca51fce1d31ecf2afa4a8d302e354ee0c18592adab10450737/cfa7e1950893a8c0248baa13d742ac0239a9c66dbf7d666f735111417852376e" "q-338853/ma-source-field/d737afc49887d8ca51fce1d31ecf2afa4a8d302e354ee0c18592adab10450737/9b4691457d839ab9262937dfb9708b845537762311d94ff190d3287b567c45da" "q-338853/ma-source-field/d737afc49887d8ca51fce1d31ecf2afa4a8d302e354ee0c18592adab10450737/6c540d83b3990efd8f637613dc939b4e11141215538eca200e7222181d6475f8" "q-338853/ma-source-field/d737afc49887d8ca51fce1d31ecf2afa4a8d302e354ee0c18592adab10450737/0a9f45c77a163a91e3677d3be9ea44cf70dad8ef14029c4c6424a95ce438e58c" "q-338853/ma-source-field/d737afc49887d8ca51fce1d31ecf2afa4a8d302e354ee0c18592adab10450737/2bba317cd311a6c4650992163cfe4c5274233afe30ad3cf6223a01396eb17f62"] :answer-field/correct "q-338853/ma-source-field/d737afc49887d8ca51fce1d31ecf2afa4a8d302e354ee0c18592adab10450737/9b4691457d839ab9262937dfb9708b845537762311d94ff190d3287b567c45da" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-338853" :question/id #uuid "4fbda52c-e557-5901-ad9a-c542b15bd0a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-338853" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-6\\lambda {y}^{′}+9{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,2y(2)+3{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=0$ and $2y(2)+3{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-6\\lambda m+9{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-3\\lambda )(m-3\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=3\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{3\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{3\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA+B\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=Bx{e}^{3\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=B{e}^{3\\lambda x}+3B\\lambda x{e}^{3\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $2y(2)+3{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2B{e}^{6\\lambda })+3(B{e}^{6\\lambda }+6B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ 4B{e}^{6\\lambda }+3B{e}^{6\\lambda }+18B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{6\\lambda }(7+18\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require $B\\ne 0.$ Thus, since ${e}^{6\\lambda }>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{7}{18}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding eigenfunction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =Bx{e}^{3\\lambda x} \\\\  & =Bx{e}^{-7x/6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-338853/ma-source-field/c8a5d3168103913d239dc385298dcfce07dca1f64b5315d078cddbe8f4416813" "q-338853/ma-source-field/d737afc49887d8ca51fce1d31ecf2afa4a8d302e354ee0c18592adab10450737"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
