 {:db/id "q-338878/ma-source-field/a810292fea0b89b4b66d0db1e458005f489c78ac2a05093be1983c8806ea893f" :answer-field/id #uuid "7184a2d1-142e-5a80-b6b3-2020f6f8c935" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/choices ["q-338878/ma-source-field/a810292fea0b89b4b66d0db1e458005f489c78ac2a05093be1983c8806ea893f/9af19e145aa88b942851773b5b6a31f32e07a58caf3c431f3ba162c6bfa4a0db" "q-338878/ma-source-field/a810292fea0b89b4b66d0db1e458005f489c78ac2a05093be1983c8806ea893f/21db0c8ca9c465ab3726d776a7cb92cb1c12957887d4f2cf603108bedfebbf8a" "q-338878/ma-source-field/a810292fea0b89b4b66d0db1e458005f489c78ac2a05093be1983c8806ea893f/b84988bb911db3e6f3547be3747a13381858ed64720c1e1d495a621d4d002a97" "q-338878/ma-source-field/a810292fea0b89b4b66d0db1e458005f489c78ac2a05093be1983c8806ea893f/3cca3fa3537838e6a5d0d30fe92c8c8eeb37955af9a5daddd94e58b9528ef66c"] :answer-field/correct "q-338878/ma-source-field/a810292fea0b89b4b66d0db1e458005f489c78ac2a05093be1983c8806ea893f/9af19e145aa88b942851773b5b6a31f32e07a58caf3c431f3ba162c6bfa4a0db" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-338878" :question/id #uuid "8eb48225-775a-576c-bf42-f55c3f321d8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-338878" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-2y=4x,\\,y(0)=1,\\,y(1)=3,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be fundamental solutions of the form $y={e}^{mx}$ to the corresponding homogeneous BVP, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below **without calculating the solution** to this BVP.\n\n$\\,$ The **absolute value** of $det(A)$ is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the corresponding homogeneous BVP {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, our inhomogeneous BVP {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP)\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $P,Q,$ and $f$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nThe inhomogeneous BVP has at most one solution if and only if the associated homogeneous BVP has only the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Specifically:\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has the unique solution $y≡0,$ then the inhomogeneous problem has a unique solution.\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has (infinitely many) nontrivial solutions, then the inhomogeneous problem either has infinitely many solutions or no solution.\n\nTo classify the solutions of the corresponding homogeneous BVP, we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe homogeneous BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ In other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the homogeneous BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Dirichlet boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=1,{\\beta }_{i}=0,a=0,$ and $b=1,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-m-2 & =0 \\\\ (m-2)(m+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=2$ and $m=-1,$ and so the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ {e}^{2} & {e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|{e}^{-1}-{e}^{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{e}-{e}^{2}| \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the corresponding homogeneous BVP $\\text{has the unique solution }y≡0.$\n\nTherefore, since the corresponding homogeneous BVP has only the trivial solution $y≡0$, our inhomogeneous BVP $\\text{has a unique solution}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-338878/ma-source-field/fe3a36452652d61dd5eb50f0204249b98391a67528d584708541c15baa25a148" "q-338878/ma-source-field/a190540aef7e4781d9c0863428ec02ec5ee8c7f19037ee90afbfcb9e3f19f68d" "q-338878/ma-source-field/a810292fea0b89b4b66d0db1e458005f489c78ac2a05093be1983c8806ea893f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
